            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-extended/pam/libpam-no-ipmi-rmcp/pam.d/common-password",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "The original \"common-password\" had a lot of statements like `[success=ok default=die]` rather than `required`.  I'm not a PAM expert so I'm surprised to see so much change when we disable IPMI; why?\n\nI'm going to have to add someone who has done more PAM stuff in to review this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-extended/pam/libpam-no-ipmi-rmcp/pam.d/common-password",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "These one word keywords are aliases to extended expressions like `[success=ok default=die]`. But I think you're right, it's better to keep the same style.\n\nNote, I use \"[success=1 default=die]\" for pam_unix.so, instead of the original one to preserve the same logic (it was used for ipmi_save) - this line means - if the module (pam_unix.so) return success, it will jump 1 module, so we're skipping the deny on pam_unix.so success."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-extended/pam/libpam-no-ipmi-rmcp/pam.d/common-password",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
